problem:
Problem description
- There is a single-product production and workforce planning problem over a finite planning horizon of $$T$$ monthly periods, indexed by $$t \in \{1,2,\dots,T\}$$.
- The product is a finished good sold to satisfy monthly demand. Demand in each period is exogenous and given by a demand profile $$d_t$$.
- The company chooses, in each period $$t$$, how much to produce in-house, how much to outsource as finished units, how many workers to employ, how many workers to hire, how many workers to fire, and how many overtime hours to use.
- The planning goal is to maximize total net profit over the horizon.
- Revenue is earned from units sold at a unit sales price $$r$$.
- In-house production consumes raw materials at a unit cost $$c^{\mathrm{mat}}$$ and requires labor time.
- Outsourced units are purchased at a unit outsourcing cost $$c^{\mathrm{out}}$$ and are available as finished goods.
- Inventory remaining at the end of each period incurs an inventory holding cost $$h$$ per unit.
- Unfulfilled demand is carried forward as backorders and incurs a backorder penalty cost $$b$$ per unit per period.
- Each worker provides regular labor capacity of $$H^{\mathrm{reg}}$$ hours per period, and regular wages are paid at rate $$w^{\mathrm{reg}}$$ per hour regardless of whether all regular hours are fully utilized.
- Overtime is allowed, paid at rate $$w^{\mathrm{ot}}$$ per hour, and the total overtime hours in any period are limited by the workforce size and the per-worker overtime allowance $$H^{\mathrm{ot}}$$.
- Each unit produced in-house requires $$\ell$$ labor hours.
- Workforce can change from one period to the next through hiring and firing, with hiring cost $$c^{\mathrm{hire}}$$ per worker and firing cost $$c^{\mathrm{fire}}$$ per worker.
- The plan starts from an initial workforce level $$W_0$$ and an initial inventory level $$I_0$$. An initial backorder level $$B_0$$ may also be specified.
- Demand fulfillment in each period uses available starting inventory, current in-house production, and outsourced units. Any unmet demand becomes backorder and is carried into the next period.
- The ending inventory at the end of the horizon must be at least $$I_T^{\min}$$.
- All backorders must be cleared by the end of the horizon, so the final backorder level must be zero.
- A solution is represented by:
  - $$x_t$$: in-house production quantity in period $$t$$
  - $$u_t$$: outsourced quantity in period $$t$$
  - $$W_t$$: workforce level in period $$t$$
  - $$H_t$$: number of workers hired in period $$t$$
  - $$F_t$$: number of workers fired in period $$t$$
  - $$o_t$$: overtime hours used in period $$t$$
  - $$I_t$$: ending inventory in period $$t$$
  - $$B_t$$: ending backorders in period $$t$$

Input Format
{
  "sets": {
    "periods": {
      "symbol": "$$\\mathcal{T}=\\{1,2,\\dots,T\\}$$",
      "description": "Set of planning periods indexed by $$t$$."
    }
  },
  "time_series": {
    "demand": {
      "symbol": "$$d_t$$",
      "shape": "$$T$$",
      "description": "Forecast demand in period $$t$$."
    }
  },
  "initial_states": {
    "initial_workforce": {
      "symbol": "$$W_0$$",
      "description": "Workforce available at the start of period $$1$$."
    },
    "initial_inventory": {
      "symbol": "$$I_0$$",
      "description": "Inventory available at the start of period $$1$$."
    },
    "initial_backorders": {
      "symbol": "$$B_0$$",
      "description": "Backorders carried into period $$1$$."
    }
  },
  "economic_parameters": {
    "sales_price": {
      "symbol": "$$r$$",
      "description": "Unit revenue earned per unit sold."
    },
    "inhouse_material_cost": {
      "symbol": "$$c^{\\mathrm{mat}}$$",
      "description": "Unit raw material cost for each in-house unit."
    },
    "outsourcing_cost": {
      "symbol": "$$c^{\\mathrm{out}}$$",
      "description": "Unit cost of each outsourced finished unit."
    },
    "inventory_holding_cost": {
      "symbol": "$$h$$",
      "description": "Holding cost per unit of ending inventory in each period."
    },
    "backorder_cost": {
      "symbol": "$$b$$",
      "description": "Penalty cost per unit of ending backorders in each period."
    },
    "regular_wage_per_hour": {
      "symbol": "$$w^{\\mathrm{reg}}$$",
      "description": "Regular wage rate per hour."
    },
    "overtime_wage_per_hour": {
      "symbol": "$$w^{\\mathrm{ot}}$$",
      "description": "Overtime wage rate per hour."
    },
    "hiring_cost_per_worker": {
      "symbol": "$$c^{\\mathrm{hire}}$$",
      "description": "Cost to hire one worker."
    },
    "firing_cost_per_worker": {
      "symbol": "$$c^{\\mathrm{fire}}$$",
      "description": "Cost to fire one worker."
    }
  },
  "operating_parameters": {
    "labor_hours_per_inhouse_unit": {
      "symbol": "$$\\ell$$",
      "description": "Labor hours required to produce one in-house unit."
    },
    "regular_hours_per_worker": {
      "symbol": "$$H^{\\mathrm{reg}}$$",
      "description": "Regular working hours supplied by one worker in each period."
    },
    "max_overtime_hours_per_worker": {
      "symbol": "$$H^{\\mathrm{ot}}$$",
      "description": "Maximum overtime hours allowed per worker in each period."
    }
  },
  "terminal_requirements": {
    "minimum_ending_inventory": {
      "symbol": "$$I_T^{\\min}$$",
      "description": "Minimum required inventory at the end of period $$T$$."
    },
    "ending_backorders": {
      "symbol": "$$0$$",
      "description": "Required backorders at the end of period $$T$$."
    }
  }
}

Output Format
{
  "production_in_house": {
    "symbol": "$$x_t$$",
    "shape": "$$T$$",
    "description": "In-house production quantity in each period $$t$$."
  },
  "outsourcing": {
    "symbol": "$$u_t$$",
    "shape": "$$T$$",
    "description": "Outsourced finished quantity in each period $$t$$."
  },
  "workforce": {
    "symbol": "$$W_t$$",
    "shape": "$$T+1$$",
    "description": "Workforce level at the start or end state of each period, including the initial state $$W_0$$."
  },
  "hires": {
    "symbol": "$$H_t$$",
    "shape": "$$T$$",
    "description": "Number of workers hired in each period $$t$$."
  },
  "fires": {
    "symbol": "$$F_t$$",
    "shape": "$$T$$",
    "description": "Number of workers fired in each period $$t$$."
  },
  "overtime_hours": {
    "symbol": "$$o_t$$",
    "shape": "$$T$$",
    "description": "Overtime hours used in each period $$t$$."
  },
  "ending_inventory": {
    "symbol": "$$I_t$$",
    "shape": "$$T+1$$",
    "description": "Inventory level at the end of each period, including the initial inventory $$I_0$$."
  },
  "ending_backorders": {
    "symbol": "$$B_t$$",
    "shape": "$$T+1$$",
    "description": "Backorder level at the end of each period, including the initial backorders $$B_0$$."
  },
  "total_net_profit": {
    "symbol": "$$\\Pi$$",
    "shape": "$$1$$",
    "description": "Maximum net profit achieved over the full planning horizon."
  }
}

Example(s)

Input 1
{
  "T": 6,
  "periods": ["January", "February", "March", "April", "May", "June"],
  "initial_workforce": 1000,
  "initial_inventory": 15000,
  "initial_backorders": 0,
  "sales_price": 300,
  "inhouse_material_cost": 90,
  "outsourcing_cost": 200,
  "inventory_holding_cost": 15,
  "backorder_cost": 35,
  "labor_hours_per_inhouse_unit": 5,
  "regular_hours_per_worker": 160,
  "regular_wage_per_hour": 30,
  "max_overtime_hours_per_worker": 20,
  "overtime_wage_per_hour": 40,
  "hiring_cost_per_worker": 5000,
  "firing_cost_per_worker": 8000,
  "minimum_ending_inventory": 10000,
  "ending_backorders": 0,
  "demand": [20000, 40000, 42000, 35000, 19000, 18500]
}
